{
  "label": "apparent_contradiction",
  "factor": "other",
  "rationale": "Both extractions are faithful to their sources. Side A reports that a three-week high-carbohydrate/low-fat diet significantly increased triglycerides within physically active adults (76 to 104 mg/dL; p = 0.005); side B reports clinically meaningful improvement (reduction) in triglycerides with a low-carbohydrate (<50 g), energy-restricted diet in people with type 2 diabetes (all p < 0.0001). The abstract-level directions for triglycerides are opposite. The sources themselves describe materially different interventions along the same dietary-carbohydrate axis: A tested a high-carbohydrate/low-fat diet for three weeks in healthy active adults, while B tested a low-carbohydrate, energy-restricted diet for 12 weeks in type 2 diabetes. This text-supported difference in exposure (plus population and duration) could explain the opposite triglyceride directions, so the pair is an apparent contradiction rather than a true one. A secondary limitation is that side B's contrast type (within-group change vs between-group vs TAU) is not specified in the abstract, but the extraction honestly flags this and it does not establish a material extraction error.",
  "diagnostics": {
    "material_extraction_error": false,
    "error_sides": [],
    "error_fields": [],
    "uncertain_extraction": false,
    "comparison_sufficient": true,
    "source_evidence": [
      {
        "side": "a",
        "sentence_id": 7,
        "quote": "Total cholesterol and LDL-cholesterol significantly decreased after the HC diet (97.9 ± 33.6 mg/dL at baseline to 78.2 ± 23.5 mg/dL; p = 0.02) while triglycerides significantly increased (76 ± 38 mg/dL at baseline to 104 ± 44 mg/dL; p = 0.005)."
      },
      {
        "side": "a",
        "sentence_id": 2,
        "quote": "The present study investigated the impact of a three-week period of HC/low-fat (HC) diet followed by a three-week wash-out-phase and subsequent LC diet on the parameters of physical capacity assessed via cardiopulmonary exercise testing, body composition via bioimpedance analysis and blood profiles, which were assessed after each of the respective diet periods."
      },
      {
        "side": "b",
        "sentence_id": 4,
        "quote": "These reductions occurred concurrently with clinically meaningful improvements in hemoglobin A1C, anthropometrics, blood pressure, and triglycerides (all p < 0.0001)."
      },
      {
        "side": "b",
        "sentence_id": 2,
        "quote": "We conducted a 12-week pragmatic, community-based parallel-group randomized controlled trial (ClinicalTrials.gov: NCT03181165) evaluating the effect of a low-carbohydrate (<50 g), energy-restricted diet (~850-1100 kcal/day; Pharm-TCR; n = 98) compared to treatment-as-usual (TAU; n = 90), delivered by community pharmacists, on glucose-lowering medication use, cardiometabolic health, and health-related quality of life."
      }
    ]
  ,
    "notes": "Side B's comparison.type is 'unclear' because the abstract does not specify whether the triglyceride improvement is a within-group change or a between-group difference versus TAU; this is a diagnostic limitation, not a material extraction error. Factor 'other' is used because the decisive supported contextual difference is the opposing dietary exposure itself (high-carbohydrate/low-fat vs low-carbohydrate energy-restricted), together with differing populations (physically active adults vs type 2 diabetes) and durations (3 vs 12 weeks)."
  }
}
